 1 big thing: PC&#x27;s AI reckoning 
 
 By Pete Gannon 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Intel sent investors scrambling with a stark warning about the sharpening slowdown in the personal computer market.

 Why it matters: AI is helping fuel the slowdown — driving up the price of PCs and competing for crucial IT budgets — and causing an industrywide rethink for what personal computers may look like down the road.

 The big picture: The PCs most of us know today — used primarily for web browsing, running software and other everyday tasks — could look radically different in an AI-driven world.

 "How we think about the personal computer will very likely change," Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said in June while unveiling a new chip platform for an AI-powered Windows PC. That sentiment was echoed yesterday on Intel's earnings call, with Intel CFO David Zinsner saying the PC "is going to go through a renaissance despite this [slowdown]." State of play: Today, PC sales are deteriorating quickly.

 Intel expects worldwide PC shipments to fall about 12% this year, followed by another roughly 10% decline in 2027. The company, which supplies processors to PC makers like Dell and HP, said Thursday that the pace of that PC "correction" has accelerated over the last two months, and predicted that the market over the next few quarters will be "challenging." 📉 The impact: Intel's stock plummeted over 28%, while Dell, HP and Lenovo fell 10.7%, 9.6% and 7.4%, respectively.

 The intrigue: PCs are already evolving.

 Intel's sales of processors for AI-capable PCs grew 35% in the third quarter compared to a year ago, even as revenue across its broader client computing business fell 5%. Lenovo says it has about a quarter of the global AI PC market and is developing personal AI agents designed to work across multiple devices. HP said AI PCs accounted for 46% of its unit shipments last quarter, a share the company expects to reach 60%–70% next year. The bottom line: "Before you get to the future you have to live through the present," Bernstein's Stacy Rasgon wrote Friday in a note about Intel.

 Those words could ring true for the PC industry, too. Go deeper 

 
 
