 2. AI&#x27;s big, ugly week 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: FactSet; Chart: Axios/Matt Phillips A flood of AI news this week failed to shore up investor confidence in the investment boom driving both the economy and markets, Axios' Matt Phillips writes. 

 Why it matters: There's basically one story in the markets, and it's AI.

 The S&amp;P 500's information technology sector — plus hyperscalers Meta, Amazon and Alphabet — now account for over 50% of the market capitalization of the index. Catch up quick: On Monday, Anthropic's much-awaited investment prospectus arrived, starting the countdown on its IPO, and Meta announced a $95 billion share offering.

 Wednesday, Microsoft and Alphabet — previously considered perhaps the most stable players in the AI frenzy — reported quarterly earnings. Yesterday, Amazon added its quarterly update to the pile, as did Intel, which has been increasingly swept up in the boom because of growing demand for its CPUs. State of play: Essentially, every update investors received this week seemed only to stimulate fresh questions and doubt about the boom.

 The large-cap, tech-heavy Nasdaq 100 stumbled to its third straight weekly loss. So did themed AI ETFs like First Trust Bloomberg Artificial Intelligence. The SOX semiconductor index notched its fourth straight week in the red. Yes, but: If there was a winner among the AI-pilled parts of the tech sector this week, it was likely Microsoft, which was basically flat for the week after reporting strong results on Tuesday.

 Unlike its hyperscaling rivals, the results showed that Microsoft is still able to generate positive cash flow thanks, in part, to remarkable stability in its core cloud computing business. The bottom line: The market's AI issues remain far from resolved.

 
 
