 4. Morning troll 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 SpaceX CEO Elon Musk ( left ) and Binance co-founder Changpeng Zhao. Photos: Britta Pedersen/Pool/AFP via Getty Images; Zed Jameson/Bloomberg via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Nothing screams Friday like a public spat between billionaires, Pete writes .

 Driving the news: Binance co-founder Changpeng Zhao didn't much appreciate Elon Musk's lack of sensitivity around the $6.3 billion hack on the crypto platform. 🥊 Zoom in: While details of the scale of the attack trickled out last night, Zhao, known as CZ, posted an assurance that Binance would cover the losses.

 A user replied by pasting an old tweet from convicted FTX chief Sam Bankman-Fried assuring the public that his exchange had the capital to cover customer losses as FTX came apart. (Spoiler alert: It did not.) To that, Musk replied with a grimacing face emoji 😬 — the social media equivalent of yelling "BURN!" while tensions flare at recess. CZ shot back at Musk, saying it was a "bad time to troll," before casually mentioning that Binance controlled over $1 billion in stock in Musk's SpaceX. 💰 The latest: Binance this afternoon said it has set aside the full amount in company funds to cover the lost assets, 80% of which are in the exact impacted token assets. It said it is "actively buying the remaining relevant token assets through market and OTC channels."

 
 
